ore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3e72fc-aba8-50b4-a971-b6bbcd848924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342912} {:answer/id #uuid "2367f65d-004f-5796-b790-b734c9dca68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342913} {:answer/id #uuid "613fdb22-0ab7-5efb-ad6c-c90d61ce1acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342914} {:answer/id #uuid "7abe2d3d-c45a-5244-8841-72981651e3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342915} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc30c5-8a2a-57fc-9e25-ba3be283de78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc30c5-8a2a-57fc-9e25-ba3be283de78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342916} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ef41485-5b71-5b7d-93e3-f8fa416ddd92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "063c389f-2fc3-5dc6-9de5-5081febe5fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342290" :question/problem "$$\n(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88980d21-935e-54c5-99ba-169928576e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342919} {:answer/id #uuid "a0aeb3b5-955e-5086-a3a9-ae02e7bed28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342920} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe7e8e6-0b6f-588c-b4d2-1fa5f36851b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342921} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab77526-2219-5be0-bcb5-485b389ce9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342922} {:answer/id #uuid "198b79f0-7c8e-5800-913f-506c51b61e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88980d21-935e-54c5-99ba-169928576e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342919} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43d43fa-9e98-5969-b1df-7578f6a280fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6fe8bd2-84b4-5110-99b1-73e60ad5751a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342295" :question/problem "$$\n(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3)(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=1.$ So, $x=1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c74dc7d-d62d-5323-9eea-8e631036ceba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342926} {:answer/id #uuid "da1768b6-d22a-584e-9aa2-f374c940b8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342927} {:answer/id #uuid "73ad6a66-82d7-5d88-9cb2-6592d9fdea06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342928} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ffc1cc-4a2b-58d9-b88e-8a4cf4c25c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342929} {:answer/id #uuid "259ed74f-c983-57bf-9755-02b8e99d7df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da1768b6-d22a-584e-9aa2-f374c940b8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342927} :answer-field/id #uuid "885437a8-8c86-5438-af5f-91dfea18a06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93434d77-9793-5722-9128-816bbd24bf8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342296" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2)(x-1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=0$\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2)(x-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-2,$ since both denominators at $x=-2$ equal zero. So, $x=-2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=1,$ since both denominators at $x=1$ equal zero. So, $x=1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3ec7b99-6a59-5c94-bc12-2134c17b070d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342933} {:answer/id #uuid "36504f54-3878-50b5-925f-357951e1ba96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342934} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb30b04-45c0-58ea-97aa-f653427c0d35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342935} {:answer/id #uuid "2d071e2a-a1ee-5fac-a816-15fc3fec85a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342936} {:answer/id #uuid "86174651-feb6-5cfc-bbf1-65fbc6fc84a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d071e2a-a1ee-5fac-a816-15fc3fec85a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342936} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d0192d7-ba54-5eb9-b607-2896ece847ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ad53237-ec99-5cc1-9169-b6a88cc54231" :question/math-academy-id "q-342299" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a529a3b1-c5e2-54c1-9cd8-ff4497c00da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342940} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5a6a7d-8ed2-5030-aa03-7b4789e5918e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342941} {:answer/id #uuid "49b6e1e9-5404-53ca-8125-dc5e44416b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342942} {:answer/id #uuid "c54e562f-81b5-592f-bae3-0ab3315aad35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342943} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcb794f-ed45-519e-81f1-cb46ff878f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dcb794f-ed45-519e-81f1-cb46ff878f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342944} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ed5850-768b-5945-b0f1-995b6a3f19f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c886c20d-db01-57b5-837f-db027fe4a87f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342300" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=0,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=0$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a77e7a4-7eae-5244-bea8-5e61a8289a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342947} {:answer/id #uuid "a097c014-0d21-5ab8-a131-6f0e17a8cea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342948} {:answer/id #uuid "39053a45-8497-5718-af86-4ae812fd23d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342949} {:answer/id #uuid "4993ddf1-4ba1-5898-974f-c03c48d2fbe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} {:answer/id #uuid "b03742f0-51c4-5956-85a6-e4350a0e5299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03742f0-51c4-5956-85a6-e4350a0e5299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342951} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddee356-ab9a-5884-a0e2-ae99775f1637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d21da441-5192-5db2-a0e2-e90c0bb5c1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-342303" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The functions $(x+1)P(x)$ and $(x+1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The point $x=-1$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1,$ multiplying them by $(x+1)$ and $(x+1)^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00aa35d1-5094-5d01-be03-24f6302453e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342954} {:answer/id #uuid "93cc824e-ad5c-5aa8-8bb3-54f3f0aa6952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342955} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb492c7-72ff-5ccf-a349-c182870966a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342956} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad6e103-713f-54a2-8fc3-b2334dbac23e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342957} {:answer/id #uuid "24d8e7ec-f262-5663-9082-a272810fa9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93cc824e-ad5c-5aa8-8bb3-54f3f0aa6952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342955} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcf657e-6d3d-5982-a00f-930819b55d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5935deed-e890-5f0a-8031-fb6be5bc3579" :question/math-academy-id "q-342367" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cb8c503-e586-5373-bea6-a178ea77cc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342961} {:answer/id #uuid "1269ec15-69d2-50fc-85d8-5aafbd97fa40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342962} {:answer/id #uuid "25debacc-7ee2-54f2-9c23-779754963f0