.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342969} :answer-field/id #uuid "d898f911-334c-5055-8d6b-c8c6cf92c047" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342968} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ce08b76-d7e6-5c52-bcbb-0e4c117afa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342973} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c07e82-2b31-5506-b92b-201eea7e5791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342974} {:answer/id #uuid "11348acf-2ccb-5e08-93be-a959cfbf93ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11348acf-2ccb-5e08-93be-a959cfbf93ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342975} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf56b2ce-08c5-5a28-940d-068c1c5c08de" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf12a8b3-3c24-5613-ab08-847e6fe908f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342421" :question/problem "$$\n(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=4$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1)(x+2)(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+2}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=4.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-2,\\,x=1,\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Thus, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)^{2}(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=4,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=(x-4)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=4.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ffa458-2062-573b-8d33-19cbc14a72f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342978} {:answer/id #uuid "b3632dc9-1d5d-5138-9d02-e9d095027885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342979} {:answer/id #uuid "26f57c6b-8735-5304-b64f-7ac7d82013de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63ffa458-2062-573b-8d33-19cbc14a72f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342978} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb437e84-0a68-51a8-bf66-4a672ebbbd86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8d26731-bd03-5a80-aaff-6528a17a7deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342982} {:answer/id #uuid "d0503b38-1237-51b3-b240-92b1475c44c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342983} {:answer/id #uuid "08e64af8-1f39-5467-8f8a-dcee016edf10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e64af8-1f39-5467-8f8a-dcee016edf10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342984} :answer-field/id #uuid "977e28e2-ce6e-5bf9-9a13-009948567c54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342981} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f761d4f-ba90-51f4-92ce-78884faf5b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342986} {:answer/id #uuid "c2261438-198c-5a2f-9966-a11090b45376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342987} {:answer/id #uuid "97b66cd8-6f87-5356-a5f5-5a8d874d2973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97b66cd8-6f87-5356-a5f5-5a8d874d2973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342988} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b54c69-96c7-5061-8d88-4c8461fe46ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b701af2e-247f-536e-9453-0ce5bb66fe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342990} {:answer/id #uuid "887fc55b-23f6-5bb6-9107-c65373cf55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342991} {:answer/id #uuid "e39bae1e-7f6c-510b-8246-8dbffcf4c392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887fc55b-23f6-5bb6-9107-c65373cf55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342991} :answer-field/id #uuid "12a598f1-969c-575d-ba5d-b42e4f97297e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1caf1bbe-a462-5b0e-b414-1ffe7c759c9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342435" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{x(x-1)}{x+3}{y}^{′}+(x+3){e}^{x}\\,y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{x(x-1)}{x+3}{y}^{′}+(x+3){e}^{x}\\,y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x+3)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-3.$ Thus, $x=-3$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{(x-1){e}^{x}}{x},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87f976e6-4525-5276-995d-045affafdac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342995} {:answer/id #uuid "40f032b7-7616-5823-853b-5249c0722039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342996} {:answer/id #uuid "e772fd98-0e1e-567d-aeef-87667968c5e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87f976e6-4525-5276-995d-045affafdac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342995} :answer-field/id #uuid "414562e9-3a5c-5a04-89dc-93012901ad9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c5a455-c7ab-5e2e-9dd2-204517fc2f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342999} {:answer/id #uuid "21c6e277-c2ce-5918-a9db-99e4d98101d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343000} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b25abf-1ee4-58ee-a4ac-c7f275d3df70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8b25abf-1ee4-58ee-a4ac-c7f275d3df70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343001} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40d3cbd-d1f4-5c4d-aa7f-d460d41ee135" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7f4c32-0d9f-5a04-b3b7-f45a458c7b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343003} {:answer/id #uuid "c90c6588-8f3d-5298-be09-8b9706bb7537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343004} {:answer/id #uuid "f6140130-9e3b-50b8-bac4-21bf92fd5a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f4c32-0d9f-5a04-b3b7-f45a458c7b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343003} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf6e8f0-25e6-5898-beaf-f8e591b45ee9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e26114-6a23-55b9-9925-1c8c1c1c47fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343007} {:answer/id #uuid "77c7b723-cc0d-554f-ad17-492adf4b3cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343008} {:answer/id #uuid "59edb6b7-3331-51ff-9aeb-48604d2a76f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77c7b723-cc0d-554f-ad17-492adf4b3cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343008} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2f0cd5-6f0b-571f-9dab-e00ce6add106" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33389159-3f75-5b82-b87a-bf3acf44ba0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342438" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=7$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=7.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}=\\frac{(x+1){e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=7,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-7)P(x)=(x-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=7.$ Therefore, $x=7$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fca9641-c958-573c-87fd-21bd825bf60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343012} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2260d7-1bd0-5fea-8405-43931a9a42fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343013} {:answer/id #uuid "415b2b17-29b4-5597-8a35-71e9e4c5b60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343014} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b65824-dcf2-5d86-bf16-a638209b9522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343015} {:answer/id #uuid "9949e354-b210-56da-b3e0-c18b7250367a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fca9641-c958-573c-87fd-21bd825bf60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343012} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a01b36-d5db-5e73-88c5-d91847dd22a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38426d90-d9de-5708-ba65-07387f5cbef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342479" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The point $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x)=(x-5)\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{2x}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=5,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}Q(x)=(x-5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{(x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96aec39-a6fc-56ea-905d-f16762114f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343019} {:answer/id #uuid "f71f9a46-5e2b-5d41-82e5-00a662ebfa97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343020} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f788ea-4c72-584c-bd44-a664d88b7202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71f9a46-5e2b-5d41-82e5-00a662ebfa97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343020} :answer-field/id #uuid "693936ec-5be5-5ae2-bf41-449f97d45170" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c6987d-7cac-5292-bd17-d8b4a411ae07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343023} {:answer/id #uuid "805f81e6-5c29-5aef-aad8-75b5db8f4d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343024} {:answer/id #uuid "7021c175-2247-58a4-b673-435a798dd75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7021c175-2247-58a4-b673-435a798dd75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343025} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe45fe1-b5d4-5fa1-a678-d54596aa8b2b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32437afc-1511-5ed2-b20e-3ae03fa4456c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343027} {:answer/id #uuid "504666d4-cfa2-5ced-b29a-9de735290ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343028} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8fb01f-352a-5f1a-9488-9fa20d0f857d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32437afc-1511-5ed2-b20e-3ae03fa4456c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343027} :answer-field/id #uuid "89680d7d-ad02-5a5d-9a85-48e9a8418015" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b9c2fe-0224-57f5-a892-ee2a5ed63ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343031} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1f396d-67c8-56a2-b07a-602f8ba64aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "c90ebe38-e5d9-518a-ad15-5a1901886921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c90ebe38-e5d9-518a-ad15-5a1901886921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343033} :answer-field/id #uuid "089440cb-4f93-51b4-b221-b3ea78625f65" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04c6ddbf-a561-50d8-a014-5a2210f9d08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342643" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=6$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-6)(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=6,$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=-2,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x}{(x-6)(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Therefore, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=6,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)P(x)=(x-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=6,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}Q(x)=(x-6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x-6}{x(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=6.$ Therefore, $x=6$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Points for a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7483e06-54fb-565f-ae71-6c19fa0baa27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343036} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a77003-7c32-51a4-ab2e-76ed0b0002d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343037} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b18e64-9508-528a-9276-7ea678a4a7fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a77003-7c32-51a4-ab2e-76ed0b0002d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343037} :answer-field/id #uuid "7567276f-b90a-5b0a-98bc-3475932699bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853e80c9-4523-58cb-b618-ce7326c1c9e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343040} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c44f04-36fc-566a-a115-26d2f5f402a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343041} {:answer/id #uuid "0793f8e7-5951-5a32-b981-1bd6eed16836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "853e80c9-4523-58cb-b618-ce7326c1c9e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343040} :answer-field/id #uuid "df8a8f08-9658-56f0-8726-18eaa2642880" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f866b0-ea30-5a8e-8c16-6b14092f9464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343044} {:answer/id #uuid "4a345818-3bd5-5e9a-9861-a9d2a2950038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343045} {:answer/id #uuid "178eb3d0-e310-59d6-a7de-06511242bc21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f866b0-ea30-5a8e-8c16-6b14092f9464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343044} :answer-field/id #uuid "e47829ef-1a46-5bd2-a426-67ae98a599d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc3b7941-0ba0-557b-a22c-5e87a9eaea9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343048} {:answer/id #uuid "a32343e9-9931-521e-b033-eff81e3fdced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343049} {:answer/id #uuid "c07c711e-988f-5756-80dc-7b8a5960dc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07c711e-988f-5756-80dc-7b8a5960dc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343050} :answer-field/id #uuid "f144b050-a4b4-5469-b0f4-4253dbd22473" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d39c521-092e-5b02-9c9d-873965d15a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342644" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-9)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-9=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=9$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=9.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1,$ so is *not* analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=9,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)P(x)=(x-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=9,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)^{2}Q(x)=(x-9)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}=(x-9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=9.$ Therefore, $x=9$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation."}]}
